{
  "heading": "Signal-driven UI",
  "notApplicable": ["ts", "py", "php", "java", "groovy", "cs", "fs", "kt", "rs", "go", "dart", "rb", "lua", "hpp"],
  "body": "Signals (events that a service publishes) replace event code that you connect by hand between services. The form saves a name through UsersClient and publishes the answer, a UserSaved model from client/models, through its applicationEventPublisher. The list registers a listener for UserSaved with OnEvent and adds the name to its list. The two services do not know each other, and you can add more listeners without changing the form. The listener only changes a field. It does not call the server, because the event already carries the saved name. This keeps the listener a short synchronous method, which every language accepts: a Rust event or lifecycle method that is async stops the build with TY1405. The server half is one small JavaScript controller in server/controllers: POST /users answers the saved user. A Yon handler keeps nothing in memory between requests, so a real app stores the name first, for example in a database. A controller in TypeScript, Python, PHP, Java, Groovy, C#, F#, Kotlin, Rust, Go, Dart, Ruby, Lua or C++ answers in the same way (see Controllers), so this section shows the server half only in JavaScript. JavaScript, TypeScript, Dart, Rust, Go, C#, F#, Kotlin, Swift, Ruby and C++ send the request through Tachyon's browser transport to the server of the page. In the compiled languages of this group, UsersSetup gives the client no adapter; C++ needs no Setup at all. The Java UsersSetup gives the client an HTTP adapter that answers in the page with the same shape, so this tab works before the server exists; a Java UsersSetup without the adapter would use the browser transport instead. Browser services in Swift, Kotlin, Java, C#, F#, Rust and Dart still get their own copy of a Setup each (see Client classes that call the server), so in every tab only the form uses the client. Python and Lua have no tab here, because the build refuses HTTPClient in Python and Lua Tac code with TY1308.",
